sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/trabecular-stress-alignment-wolffs-law--claude-opus-5-5--2> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Calibrate the growth-rule null and re-orientation by remodelling simulation" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/trabecular-stress-alignment-wolffs-law> ;
    dct:description "The problem asks whether alignment is stronger than a load-independent growth rule would produce, but the size of that null is unknown. Simulate trabecular architecture on 2D and coarse 3D voxel domains (a femoral-head section and a rectangular block) under three rule families: (1) a Huiskes-type remodelling rule driven by strain energy density sensed by osteocytes with spatial influence decay; (2) load-independent rules (reaction-diffusion Turing-type patterning of bone deposition, and growth along a fixed developmental field such as radial distance from an ossification centre); (3) mixed rules with a tunable load weight w from 0 to 1. For each run compute the same fabric-to-principal-stress angle statistic as approach 1. Then rotate the applied load by 13 and 20 degrees, matching the magnitudes used in the guineafowl and sheep experiments (Pontzer et al. 2006; Barak et al. 2011), continue the simulation, and record the fabric re-orientation angle and its time constant." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "A table showing that the alignment statistic separates load-driven (w = 1) from load-independent (w = 0) rules by more than the seed-to-seed spread, with the minimum load weight that produces a median angle below 20 degrees, and predicted re-orientation magnitudes for the two experimental rotations." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: If load-independent rules on the same geometry produce median angles within the seed spread of the load-driven rule, the alignment statistic cannot discriminate causes and approach 1 must be redesigned around re-orientation; if results change by more than 10 degrees with voxel resolution or VOI size, the simulation is not converged." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/simulation" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Implement the remodelling loop in Python with a voxel FE solver (FEniCSx or a sparse linear-elastic voxel solver), verify against the published qualitative outcomes of Huiskes et al. 2000 (aligned struts under uniaxial load, re-alignment after load rotation). 2. Implement the load-independent rules and the mixed-rule family; fix seeds and record all parameters. 3. For each rule and 20 seeds, compute the distribution of fabric-to-stress angles and the stress-anisotropy dependence. 4. Apply load rotation and record re-orientation angle versus simulated time. 5. Publish a lookup table: expected median angle and anisotropy slope for w in {0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1}, to be used as the pre-registered growth-rule null in approach 1." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Missing: No wet data needed; this calibrates nulls and does not by itself settle whether real bone is aligned." ;
    skos:note "Tool: python" ;
    skos:note "Tool: fenicsx" ;
    skos:note "Tool: frontier_simulate" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-05> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-06> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-07> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-16> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1038/35015116> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1016/0021-9290(87)90058-3> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1242/jeb.01971> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bone.2011.08.020> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/trabecular-stress-alignment-wolffs-law> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Do trabeculae align with principal stress, and is that alignment causal?" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1038/35015116> dct:title "Effects of mechanical forces on maintenance and adaptation of form in trabecular bone" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Huiskes et al. 2000, Effects of mechanical forces on maintenance and adaptation of form in trabecular bone" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1016/0021-9290(87)90058-3> dct:title "Trabecular bone density and loading history: Regulation of connective tissue biology by mechanical energy" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Carter, Fyhrie and Whalen 1987, Trabecular bone density and loading history: regulation of connective tissue biology by mechanical energy" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1242/jeb.01971> dct:title "Trabecular bone in the bird knee responds with high sensitivity to changes in load orientation" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Pontzer et al. 2006, Trabecular bone in the bird knee responds with high sensitivity to changes in load orientation" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bone.2011.08.020> dct:title "A Wolff in sheep's clothing: Trabecular bone adaptation in response to changes in joint loading orientation" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Barak et al. 2011, A Wolff in sheep's clothing" .
}

